[38;2;124;124;124mdual representation). Since V is self-dual (standard representation), dim V_G = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdim V^{G^T} = dim V^G (since G^T = G, as Γ(3) is closed under transposition).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo Z^n_G is finite iff V^G = 0, i.e., G has no nonzero fixed vector in Q^n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Claim:** Γ(m) has no nonzero fixed vector in Q^n for n ≥ 2, m ≥ 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof of claim: Let v ∈ Q^n with gv = v for all g ∈ Γ(m). For any i ≠ j, the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melementary matrix E_{ij}(m) = I + m·e_{ij} (where e_{ij} has 1 in position (i,j)[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand 0 elsewhere) lies in Γ(m). Then E_{ij}(m)v = v implies m·e_{ij}·v = 0. Now [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(e_{ij}·v)_i = v_j and all other components are 0, so m·v_j = 0, hence v_j = 0. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSince n ≥ 2, for each j there exists i ≠ j, so v_j = 0 for all j. Thus v = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince n ≥ 3 ≥ 2, the claim applies. Therefore V^G = 0, so dim V_G = 0, so Z^n_G [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis finite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Conclusion:** G = Γ(3) is a finite index, torsion-free subgroup of GL_n(Z) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(for n ≥ 3) such that Z^n_G is finite. The answer is **yes**.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me make sure the duality argument is correct. V_G = V / Σ_g (g-I)V. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdual: a linear functional φ ∈ V^* descends to V_G iff φ((g-I)v) = 0 for all g, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mv, iff φ ∘ (g-I) = 0 for all g, iff φ ∘ g = φ for all g, iff φ is G-invariant [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(where G acts on V^* by (g·φ)(v) = φ(g^{-1}v), so g·φ = φ means φ(g^{-1}v) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mφ(v) means φ ∘ g^{-1} = φ means φ ∘ g = φ). So (V_G)^* = (V^*)^G.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow (V^*)^G: G acts on V^* by g·φ = φ ∘ g^{-1}. In coordinates, if we identify [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mV^* with V via the standard basis (dual basis), then g acts on V^* by (g^{-1})^T[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= (g^T)^{-1}. So (V^*)^G = {v ∈ V : (g^T)^{-1} v = v ∀ g} = {v : g^T v = v ∀ g} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= V^{G^T}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo dim V_G = dim (V_G)^{**} = dim ((V_G)^*)^* ... well, for finite-dimensional [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mV, dim V_G = dim (V_G)^* = dim (V^*)^G = dim V^{G^T}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince G = Γ(3) is closed under transpose, G^T = G, so dim V_G = dim V^G = 0 by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe claim. ✓[0m

